  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 8,
    "lesson_title": "Time-Derivative Property",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert differentiation in time into multiplication by j $\\omega$ in the continuous-time Fourier transform domain to compute spectra without redoing the transform integral.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time-Derivative Property",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When you already know $X(j\\omega)$ for $x(t)$, the time-derivative property lets you get the spectrum of $x'(t)$ without redoing the transform integral. The point of the step is simple: differentiation in time becomes multiplication by $j\\omega$ in the frequency domain.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with one differentiable signal $x(t)$ whose CTFT $X(j\\omega)$ is already known. We want the transform of $x'(t)$, so write the CTFT integral for the derivative and keep the known spectrum in view.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} \\frac{d}{dt}x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt = \\left[x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\right]_{-\\infty}^{\\infty}+j\\omega\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} x(t)e^{-j\\omega t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The integration-by-parts step produces a boundary term and a frequency factor. Under the usual assumption that the boundary term vanishes, only the $j\\omega$ factor remains multiplying the original transform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}x(t)\\right\\}=j\\omega X(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This means each frequency component is scaled by its own $j\\omega$ factor. The shortcut is valid whenever the original transform pair is known and the boundary term goes to zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The operating rule for later steps is: differentiate in time first, then multiply the known spectrum by $j\\omega$ instead of re-evaluating the integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Transforming a First Derivative from a Known Spectrum",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $\\frac{d}{dt}e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ using the known transform pair for $e^{j\\omega_0 t}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "We already know the transform pair $e^{j\\omega_0 t} \\leftrightarrow 2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)$, so the derivative property lets us move straight to the frequency domain. The cue is that a time derivative is being taken on a signal whose spectrum is already known.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}e^{j\\omega_0 t}\\right\\}=j\\omega\\left(2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)\\right)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The $\\delta$ spike is supported only at $\\omega=\\omega_0$, so the $j\\omega$ factor evaluates to $j\\omega_0$ at the spike. That turns the product into a single scaled impulse.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}e^{j\\omega_0 t}\\right\\}=j\\omega_0\\,2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the derivative spectrum is the same spike at $\\omega_0$, scaled by the derivative constant $j\\omega_0$. The example shows the shortcut in its cleanest form: use the known pair, multiply by $j\\omega$, and simplify at the spike.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Applying the j $\\omega$ Factor to a Known Transform Pair",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $\\frac{d}{dt}\\cos(\\omega_0 t)$ by multiplying the known cosine spectrum by $j\\omega$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "We already know the transform pair $\\cos(\\omega_0 t) \\leftrightarrow \\pi\\bigl[\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)+\\delta(\\omega+\\omega_0)\\bigr]$, so the cue is a derivative applied to a known pair. That means we can stay in the frequency domain and multiply the cosine spectrum by $j\\omega$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\right\\}=j\\omega\\,\\pi\\bigl[\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)+\\delta(\\omega+\\omega_0)\\bigr]",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The spike at $\\omega=\\omega_0$ picks up the factor $j\\omega_0$, while the spike at $\\omega=-\\omega_0$ picks up the factor $-j\\omega_0$. That is why the two terms end up with opposite signs after simplification.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\cos(\\omega_0 t)\\right\\}=j\\omega_0\\pi\\delta(\\omega-\\omega_0)-j\\omega_0\\pi\\delta(\\omega+\\omega_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the derivative spectrum keeps the same two-sided support as the cosine, but each spike is scaled by the frequency where it lives. This is the clean two-spike version of the $j\\omega$ shortcut.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Combining Linearity with the Time-Derivative Property",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Find the CTFT of $\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(3\\cos(2t)-2\\cos(5t)\\bigr)$ by combining linearity with the derivative property.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a weighted sum inside the derivative, so split the expression first. Linearity lets you transform each cosine term separately before you recombine the pieces.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(3\\cos(2t)-2\\cos(5t)\\bigr)\\right\\}=3\\,\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\cos(2t)\\right\\}-2\\,\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\cos(5t)\\right\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "From the previous example, use $\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\cos(\\Omega t)\\right\\}=j\\Omega\\pi\\bigl[\\delta(\\omega-\\Omega)-\\delta(\\omega+\\Omega)\\bigr]$. Apply it once with $\\Omega=2$ and once with $\\Omega=5$, keeping the original coefficients $3$ and $-2$ attached.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3\\bigl(2j\\pi[\\delta(\\omega-2)-\\delta(\\omega+2)]\\bigr)-2\\bigl(5j\\pi[\\delta(\\omega-5)-\\delta(\\omega+5)]\\bigr)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now simplify the coefficients. The first pair becomes $6j\\pi[\\delta(\\omega-2)-\\delta(\\omega+2)]$, and the second pair becomes $-10j\\pi[\\delta(\\omega-5)-\\delta(\\omega+5)]$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "6j\\pi\\delta(\\omega-2)-6j\\pi\\delta(\\omega+2)-10j\\pi\\delta(\\omega-5)+10j\\pi\\delta(\\omega+5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result stays separated by frequency pair, which makes the linearity step visible. That is the spectrum of the derivative of the weighted sum without redoing any transform integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 8,
        "lesson_title": "Time-Derivative Property",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Using the Time-Derivative Property to Avoid the Transform Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Without evaluating any transform integrals, find the CTFT of $\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(2\\cos(3t)-4\\sin(2t)+e^{j5t}\\bigr)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a composite derivative built from signals with known transform pairs, so do not start an integral. Split the sum first, then apply linearity and the derivative property term by term.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\bigl(2\\cos(3t)-4\\sin(2t)+e^{j5t}\\bigr)\\right\\}=2\\,\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\cos(3t)\\right\\}-4\\,\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}\\sin(2t)\\right\\}+\\mathcal{F}\\left\\{\\frac{d}{dt}e^{j5t}\\right\\}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the known pairs from the earlier steps: $\\cos(3t)\\leftrightarrow \\pi\\bigl[\\delta(\\omega-3)+\\delta(\\omega+3)\\bigr]$, $\\sin(2t)\\leftrightarrow j\\pi\\bigl[\\delta(\\omega+2)-\\delta(\\omega-2)\\bigr]$, and $e^{j5t}\\leftrightarrow 2\\pi\\delta(\\omega-5)$. Multiplying each pair by $j\\omega$ gives the derivative spectra without any new integration.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2\\bigl(3j\\pi[\\delta(\\omega-3)-\\delta(\\omega+3)]\\bigr)-4\\bigl(2\\pi[\\delta(\\omega-2)+\\delta(\\omega+2)]\\bigr)+10j\\pi\\delta(\\omega-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The coefficients stay attached to their own frequency spikes: the cosine term becomes a signed $j\\pi$ pair, the sine term becomes a real two-spike pair, and the complex exponential becomes a single shifted spike scaled by $j5$. Now combine the pieces into one spectrum.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "6j\\pi\\delta(\\omega-3)-6j\\pi\\delta(\\omega+3)-8\\pi\\delta(\\omega-2)-8\\pi\\delta(\\omega+2)+10j\\pi\\delta(\\omega-5)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the CTFT with no transform integral at all. The only work was decomposition, reuse of known pairs, and recombination under linearity.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
